# SSSF environment. Copy to .env at the target repo root and fill in.
#
# WHICH KEYS YOU NEED DEPENDS ON YOUR ROSTER.
# An OMP agent with `pm_profile` uses that profile's configured authentication.
# An OMP agent without a profile names `provider/model-id`; its provider chooses
# the required credential. A `claude_code` agent uses the authenticated Claude
# CLI rather than an environment key written here.
#
# The stamped starter roster uses direct Claude Code for planning and named OMP
# profiles for execution lanes. Inspect `adws/adw_sssf_config/sssf.config.yaml`
# before adding keys or replacing profiles with explicit model bindings.
#
# Nothing validates credentials for you. `agents.validate()` checks an explicit
# OMP model binding against `omp models` and verifies prompt files; profile and
# provider authentication fail when the selected agent starts.

# Optional example for a roster that selects an OpenRouter-backed OMP model.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Optional runtime overrides
# OMP_PATH=omp          # OMP binary if not on PATH (PI_PATH also read)
# CLAUDE_PATH=~/.local/bin/claude
# ENGINEER_NAME=        # engineer lane label, defaults to git user.name

# Maestro provision writes these under state_root/<repo>/keys/maestro.env.
# `maestro bootstrap` creates them; source that file before finalize/start
# if the process environment is empty. Named-plan commands also read the
# same values from the keys directory when the variables are unset.
# MAESTRO_VERIFY_KEY=
# MAESTRO_SIGNING_SEED=
# MAESTRO_ROUTE_VERIFY_KEY=

